# Request Guard MCP native bundle

Bundle label: `@RELEASE_LABEL@`

Source commit: [`@SOURCE_COMMIT@`](https://github.com/@REPOSITORY@/tree/@SOURCE_COMMIT@)

This archive contains a prebuilt **inbound request classifier**. It does not
intercept website traffic or control an agent's outbound requests. A site must
send selected request metadata to the server and enforce its own policy.

Start with the bundled [installation guide](INSTALL.md). It covers checksum
and, for an approved tagged release, signed-attestation verification; token
setup; HTTP, custom WebSocket and local stdio clients; optional backends; and
the limits of each transport. Stable version tags additionally require
reviewed environment approval before publication. The archive also contains `.env.example`, the
license, `Cargo.lock` and a PostgreSQL legacy-row migration. It does not
contain the repository's build scripts, tests, deployment manifests or
companion skill. The link above points to the exact source commit for those
files. A private candidate bundle may have no matching public release tag,
and its source link may require repository access.

For HTTP or WebSocket, replace the example bearer and configure a unique
`AUTH_TOKENS` value of at least 32 characters. The server speaks plaintext
HTTP internally; put a trusted TLS proxy in front of remote clients. Local
`--stdio` relies on operating-system process access rather than a bearer.
PostgreSQL, Redis and MaxMind are optional for core `classify` and `explain`,
but individual enrichment, persistence and report tools need their backends.
Redis supports plaintext `redis://` only: use a trusted isolated link or an
independently secured loopback tunnel, or leave it unconfigured. For remote
PostgreSQL use `sslmode=verify-full` with a trusted CA.

The source commit, bundle label and checksums identify this package. Private
workflow-dispatch candidates are unpublished test artifacts; they do not
prove a public registry install, tagged provenance or stable release approval.
